From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 20:04:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] iio: qcom_smgr: push only the enabled axes

The report handler always pushes X, Y and Z of an accelerometer,
gyroscope or magnetometer sample in that order, which is the scan layout
only when all three channels are enabled. A buffer with fewer axes
enabled (in_accel_z_en alone, say) got X where it expected its first
enabled channel.

Copy just the enabled axes into the sample. The handler takes them from
a copy of the mask made when the buffer is enabled: the core frees
active_scan_mask when the buffer is disabled, and a report can still be
on its way then.

Not declaring the full mask in available_scan_masks and leaving the
demux to the core is deliberate. With the last channels of a scan off,
the core's demux takes the timestamp from the wrong offset (x+timestamp
reads the Z sample as the timestamp), in mainline as well.

The driver is in the msm8996-mainline tree (v6.19.5-msm8996), not in
mainline yet.

Tested on a Galaxy Tab S3 (SM-T820) lying still, with the whole series:
every subset of the axes, with and without the timestamp, on the
accelerometer, gyroscope and magnetometer, and the light sensor with and
without it. Records have the size the channels imply, the padding is zero
and timestamps rise at the set rate. Each axis matches a full scan on the
accelerometer and magnetometer. The gyroscope's offset moves between
buffer enables, so its values were not compared.

Signed-off-by: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c | 16 ++++++++++++----
 include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h     |  3 +++
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
index b4f016bdab..9cadbc5d89 100644
--- a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
+++ b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
@@ -268,7 +268,7 @@ static void qcom_smgr_buffering_report_handler(struct qmi_handle *hdl,
 	struct qcom_smgr_sensor *sensor;
 	struct qcom_smgr_iio_data iio_data;
 	int temp;
-	u8 i, j;
+	u8 i, j, n;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < smgr->sensor_count; ++i) {
 		sensor = &smgr->sensors[i];
@@ -289,11 +289,13 @@ static void qcom_smgr_buffering_report_handler(struct qmi_handle *hdl,
 	 * Construct data to be passed to IIO. Since we are matching report rate
 	 * with sample rate, we only get a single sample in every report. Clear
 	 * it first, so that the padding before the timestamp does not pass
-	 * stack contents to userspace.
+	 * stack contents to userspace. A report has all axes of the sensor;
+	 * a scan has only the enabled ones, with no gaps.
 	 */
 	memset(&iio_data, 0, sizeof(iio_data));
-	for (j = 0; j < ARRAY_SIZE(ind->samples[0].values); ++j)
-		iio_data.values[j] = ind->samples[0].values[j];
+	n = 0;
+	for_each_set_bit(j, &sensor->scan_mask, ARRAY_SIZE(ind->samples[0].values))
+		iio_data.values[n++] = ind->samples[0].values[j];
 
 	/*
 	 * SMGR reports sensor data in 32-bit fixed-point values, with 16 bits
@@ -339,6 +341,12 @@ static int qcom_smgr_sensor_postenable(struct iio_dev *iio_dev)
 	struct qcom_smgr_iio_priv *priv = iio_priv(iio_dev);
 	struct qcom_smgr_sensor *sensor = priv->sensor;
 
+	/*
+	 * The core frees active_scan_mask when the buffer is disabled, and a
+	 * report can still be on its way then: keep a copy for the handler.
+	 */
+	sensor->scan_mask = *iio_dev->active_scan_mask;
+
 	return qcom_smgr_request_buffering(smgr, sensor, true);
 }
 
diff --git a/include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h b/include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h
index 25ee29a323..ce726b382d 100644
--- a/include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h
+++ b/include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h
@@ -55,6 +55,9 @@ struct qcom_smgr_sensor {
 	 */
 	u8 data_type;
 
+	/* The channels enabled in the buffer, for the report handler */
+	unsigned long scan_mask;
+
 	struct iio_dev *iio_dev;
 };
 
